WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.620
NICK TAYLOR: Hello, thanks
for joining us today.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.287
We're going to be
talking about building

00:00:09.287 --> 00:00:12.200
a large-scale migration
factory in Google Cloud.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.950
My name is Nick Taylor
and I'm [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.850
lead for migration in our
professional services team.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.742
And with me today is Ben.

00:00:18.742 --> 00:00:21.650
BEN HAGAI: Hey,
everyone, Ben Hagai here.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.630
I'm the migration
global practice

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:27.980
lead for professional
services organization.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.083
NICK TAYLOR: Thanks, Ben.

00:00:29.083 --> 00:00:31.250
So we're going to talk about
three steps in building

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.710
a large-scale
migration factory in

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.118
and how to get that
infrastructure into GCP.

00:00:36.118 --> 00:00:38.160
First of those steps is
building a business case,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.785
so understanding the value of a
migration, why you're doing it,

00:00:40.785 --> 00:00:43.110
and what you're able to
achieve for your business.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.740
Step two is about
establishing foundations,

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.565
both technical
and non-technical,

00:00:47.565 --> 00:00:49.940
and preparing for scale,
picking those first applications

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.992
and getting some
infrastructure moved.

00:00:51.992 --> 00:00:54.200
And then the third step is
about scaling the factory,

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.180
so how to industrialize
the process,

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.260
move large volumes of workloads.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.510
So first off, building
that business case.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.218
Even though it's almost
inevitable you'll

00:01:02.218 --> 00:01:04.489
save money with a cloud
migration any effort--

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:07.110
any change requires effort
and that means cost.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.160
So we need to understand really
where the value realization is

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.590
and how you're going to save
money and gain efficiencies

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:13.822
in [INAUDIBLE] migration.

00:01:13.822 --> 00:01:15.530
So let's take a look
at how we understand

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.205
the value of that migration.

00:01:18.205 --> 00:01:20.330
So it's true to say that
the majority of IT budgets

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.910
are allocated today to
maintaining the status quo,

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.165
looking after your
current infrastructure.

00:01:25.165 --> 00:01:26.540
So what we need
to think about is

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.570
how we can lower your
run costs to free up

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.430
money for innovation.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.540
Migrations are
always disruptive,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.680
so it's important to get
executive sponsorship

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.100
in driving that migration.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.020
And demonstrating
those savings are

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.250
a good step in that process.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.040
So we think about building
a migration-- a return

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.720
on investment calculation.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.200
We look at your as-is run rate.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.620
So what are you spending
today on infrastructure,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.270
on maintaining your
infrastructure,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.610
and on software
licensing, on people

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.830
who manage that
infrastructure, also the assets

00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:01.610
themselves-- data centers,
land, rent, things like power?

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.740
On the other side, we
compare that with what

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.790
the likely to-be spend is.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.050
So what's the anticipated
ramp of spend in GCP?

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.410
What would it cost you to get
the migrations moved over?

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:13.910
And what does the
timeline look like

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:16.080
to decommission your
existing infrastructure?

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.620
So when will you start
to realize that value?

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.590
When we look at your
current run costs,

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.610
we think about the inventory
of your infrastructure

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.570
and understand it from a
real sort of fact-based way

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.450
where you're spending
today and how you how easy

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.758
it will be to do the migration.

00:02:31.758 --> 00:02:33.300
So we use inventory
tooling for that.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.550
You can see examples of some
of our preferred partners

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.050
on the screen.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.580
Those tools will look at
the servers themselves,

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.720
so what's their size and
shape, CPU, memory, and so on?

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.525
And also how well they utilized
as well as the dependencies

00:02:45.525 --> 00:02:46.710
in between them.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.990
This tooling will also look
at things like storage,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.307
networking, security costs.

00:02:50.307 --> 00:02:52.140
And you need to consider
things like logging

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.610
and backups as well.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.507
And we add into those
supporting costs--

00:02:55.507 --> 00:02:57.840
building maintenance, power
cooling, software licensing,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:58.560
and so on--

00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.970
as we build the cost model.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.860
Then we think about
the other side

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.600
of the business
case, which is how

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.100
you are going to realize value?

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.683
How you're going to
drive efficiencies

00:03:06.683 --> 00:03:08.387
through cloud migration?

00:03:08.387 --> 00:03:10.470
The first of these is
infrastructure optimization.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.070
And grid computing is a
great example of this.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.330
Imagine risk calculations
that run for eight hours

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.328
overnight, five days a week.

00:03:17.328 --> 00:03:18.870
So your production
environment, then,

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.720
is idle for 16 hours
a day during the week

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.025
and often the complete weekend.

00:03:23.025 --> 00:03:24.900
Meanwhile, your disaster
recovery environment

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.400
is idle the whole time.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.140
So actually you're barely
using your infrastructure.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.990
We've seen banks that
made 18% savings when

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.790
they moved those
calculations to the cloud

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.280
by just using
infrastructure on demand

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.670
without making any changes
to their applications.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.770
Although less magnified, that's
true for disaster recovery

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:41.670
in general.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.460
And you don't need a full-sized
DR environment always running.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.720
You can spin that up as
you need it in the cloud.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.720
There are other savings
here for infrastructure,

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.540
things like storage
optimization,

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.730
considering backup
strategy, and just adjusting

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.210
your infrastructure [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.710
The second thing to consider
here is operational efficiency.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:58.835
So Google Cloud gives
consolidated availability

00:03:58.835 --> 00:04:00.710
and performance
running-- monitoring

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.890
of all the infrastructure
that you've deployed.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:05.160
And, similarly, we recommend
using infrastructure

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.278
as code for deployment of
both servers themselves

00:04:08.278 --> 00:04:10.320
and all the supporting
infrastructure, firewalls,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.232
and so on.

00:04:11.232 --> 00:04:13.440
By doing so, you're realizing
significant operational

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.318
savings.

00:04:14.318 --> 00:04:16.110
Also things like
automated patch management

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.120
and consolidating
security tooling

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.500
mean that you'll save further.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.417
When you think about
modernizing applications,

00:04:21.417 --> 00:04:24.078
that's the third step here,
and refactoring applications

00:04:24.078 --> 00:04:24.870
to be cloud native.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.810
It means we'll take advantage
of both of those things, just

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.210
infrastructure when
you need it and also

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:33.240
the operational efficiency
of continuous deployment

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.138
and consolidated cloud
native [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:35.138 --> 00:04:36.930
So when we bring all
those things together,

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.110
we build a complete value model
for your cloud migration, both

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.000
the current and
future operating costs

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.830
gathered from
tooling and then data

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.640
from across the organization on
the benefits the top can bring.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.160
Together, those things
show your expected returns.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.800
You'll see here there's a bit
of a hump for migration cost,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.160
inevitably as you're dual
running infrastructure

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.252
and perhaps paying for
services, whether ours

00:04:57.252 --> 00:04:59.460
or a partner or even just
your own timing during that

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.520
migration.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.780
But we can help you offset that
migration hump as you migrate

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.490
and maximize value
as early as you can.

00:05:06.055 --> 00:05:08.610
So far we've spoken about cost
savings, but what other value

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.890
you can get by
migrating to the cloud?

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.340
Cloud migration helps you
unlock business value.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.090
So as you've migrated
data into the cloud,

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.460
often you're seeing
queries take seconds

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.840
where they used to take days.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.700
So that access to
real-time data enables

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.860
you to make more strategic
business decisions based

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.710
on real-time data and
also allows access

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.290
to that tooling, that data,
and to things like machine

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:30.840
learning across
your organization.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.460
It also enables
business agility.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.670
So we described that kind
of rapid software lifecycle

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:37.620
and continuous deployment,
and means typically lower time

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.985
to market for new features and
new innovation in the business.

00:05:42.985 --> 00:05:44.360
And then finally,
Google Cloud is

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.690
one of the most robust,
so our global security

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:50.720
and our world-class network
used for our own products

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:52.520
is now available to
you, meaning there's

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:54.890
less likelihood of business
disruption from IT service

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:56.000
outages.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.107
So you've thought about a
clear business case now.

00:05:58.107 --> 00:06:00.440
You've got approval to move
forward with your migration.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:01.550
What next?

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:03.110
So we start to look
at how to build

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:04.858
the foundations in the Cloud.

00:06:04.858 --> 00:06:06.650
As you'd expect from
Google, we think about

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:09.567
migration more than
just about technology.

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:11.900
You might need to change your
organization as well, when

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:13.983
you're thinking about
adopting the Cloud to regain

00:06:13.983 --> 00:06:15.380
the benefits.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.750
We use the Google Cloud
adoption framework

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:20.370
as the core of our operating
model in the Cloud.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.790
And how do you understand the
implications of that cloud

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:24.498
migration, how to get
the best out of it,

00:06:24.498 --> 00:06:27.080
and what might need to
happen in your organization?

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.620
The Cloud adoption framework
is made of four key themes--

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.940
learn, lead, scale, and
secure-- that you see here

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:33.028
on the screen.

00:06:33.028 --> 00:06:34.820
Learn means, of course,
learning new skills

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.070
in training and
technology, but it also

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:38.165
changes the way you learn.

00:06:38.165 --> 00:06:40.250
Cloud resources are
cheap and disposable,

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:42.590
so it means people can
learn and innovate safely

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:45.500
through hackathons
and experimentation.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.820
Leadership means, of course,
executive sponsorship

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:49.332
for the program
and a clear vision.

00:06:49.332 --> 00:06:51.290
But it also means sharing
knowledge [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:52.918
practice across
the organization.

00:06:52.918 --> 00:06:55.460
We recommend something like a
center of excellence or a Cloud

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.660
Platform team to
help achieve that

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.400
and this kind of psychological
safety and reduction of silos

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.560
across the organization as well.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:06.150
Scale means using
Cloud adoption to build

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:07.772
repeatable, scalable processes.

00:07:07.772 --> 00:07:09.980
So we talked about DevOps
and continuous integration,

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:11.930
and infrastructure
is covered here.

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.300
To me, it's much
easier for you to scale

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:15.890
vast quantities
of infrastructure

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:18.260
with less operational toil.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.255
Security means
trust, really, trust

00:07:20.255 --> 00:07:22.230
in the deployment of the Cloud.

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.830
We think typically that
Cloud security is stronger

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:26.120
than on-premise security.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:27.080
But it's different.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:31.380
You need to think about how
your security model will change.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.850
So how do you know where you
are with those four key themes?

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:36.950
We take a gamble,
and we help you

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:39.932
do a Cloud Maturity Assessment
across each of the themes.

00:07:39.932 --> 00:07:41.390
We'll look at
whether you're taking

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:43.850
a tactical, strategic, or
transformational view of Cloud

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:44.792
adoption.

00:07:44.792 --> 00:07:46.250
So where are you
today, and how far

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.650
do you want to go as an
organization, and the change

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.420
that you might
want to undertake?

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:52.760
We'll help you make
recommendations, allow

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.650
you to set up your Cloud
team, and really get

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:57.235
the best out of the Cloud
as you start [INAUDIBLE]..

00:07:57.235 --> 00:07:58.610
That assessment
can be undertaken

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.068
by asking our
professional services

00:08:00.068 --> 00:08:02.630
team, through partners,
or you can do it yourself

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:03.780
using a self-service tool.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:05.765
And the URL for that
is on the screen.

00:08:05.765 --> 00:08:07.640
So once you've understood
that Cloud Maturity

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:09.910
and where you are
on those key themes,

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:12.160
you'll really start to
understand the operating model

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.040
you want to build in the Cloud.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:16.880
So those translate into
those themes you see here

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.060
on the left of our platform
engineering and governance

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.480
and building our
operating model.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:23.480
We take the lessons from
the Maturity Assessment

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.840
and build that Cloud Operating
Model for your organization.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:28.790
It takes all those
themes and establishes

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:30.290
a set of controls
and services that

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:32.863
allow you to operate
comfortably in the Cloud.

00:08:32.863 --> 00:08:34.280
Most of that, as
I mentioned, sits

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.780
in a Cloud Center of Excellence
team or Cloud Platform Team,

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.470
but spreads out across
the organization.

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:40.918
You'll see here
on the right where

00:08:40.918 --> 00:08:43.460
that Cloud Center of Excellence
sits between your application

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:46.965
teams on the right and the Cloud
Infrastructure on the left.

00:08:46.965 --> 00:08:48.590
If you're a DevOps
team, you might just

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.370
adopt the Cloud straight
away within those guardrails.

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:53.030
But for lots of
legacy applications

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:55.310
and the focus of
discussion today, we

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:57.260
recommend the
Migration Factory team,

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.540
that set of people and
processes that can help

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.440
migrate applications at scale.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.720
So once you've built that
organizational structure,

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:05.387
we think about the
technical foundations

00:09:05.387 --> 00:09:07.730
that you need based on
those controls that you've

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:09.710
defined to automate
the deployment

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:11.190
of your infrastructure.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:14.520
So you need that secure place
to move your infrastructure

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.090
and consider whether it looks
like you're on premise estate

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:19.220
or whether it needs to
evolve, things like identity

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:22.030
management, security controls,
obviously, networking,

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:23.780
so establishing the
technical connectivity

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:25.995
between your own premise
environment and the Cloud,

00:09:25.995 --> 00:09:27.620
but also the structure
of your network,

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:29.640
whether it looks like,
again, on premise,

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.540
whether you're using
new [INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.820
Instrumentation means
defining the baseline level

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:36.620
of your monitoring of
your infrastructure,

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:38.745
making sure it's available
and performs at least as

00:09:38.745 --> 00:09:41.585
well as it did on premise
when you move it to the Cloud.

00:09:41.585 --> 00:09:43.460
And finally, of course,
it's really important

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:44.840
to think about cost control.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.007
It used to be that your
procurement department would

00:09:47.007 --> 00:09:48.920
buy infrastructure or
approve that perhaps

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.810
once a year, whereas
in the cloud,

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:51.910
that's much more dynamic.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:54.410
So understanding the impact of
that across your organization

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:55.940
is really important.

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:59.510
Remember, our focus to this
is as infrastructure as code.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:01.550
We use our Cloud
Foundations Toolkit

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:04.160
as a common baseline set
of controls and automation

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:07.010
for deploying all of
that configuration.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.130
It allows you to rapidly
deploy infrastructure, but also

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:12.530
all of the supporting
guardrails and controls.

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:14.810
Everything is then virtually
controled and really easy

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:16.000
to deploy.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:18.000
If you choose not to do
that in an automated way

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.608
when you first get
started in GCP,

00:10:19.608 --> 00:10:21.650
we also have an enterprise
on-boarding checklist,

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:23.600
which is a step-by-step
manual process

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:26.660
to help you establish similar
controls for your environment.

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:29.450
Once you've got that
environment all set up,

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:31.800
we help you evaluate
the migration path

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.300
for each of your workloads.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.150
We have an overall approach
that's really four steps here--

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:39.420
assess, plan,
migrate, and optimize.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.150
When we think about migration,
there are two key approaches.

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:43.920
For that re-hosting
and re-platforming,

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:46.230
we use our migration
factory approach, the focus

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.760
of our discussion today.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.930
But we also think about
refactoring applications

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:52.710
and how you change
and modernize them.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:55.350
So when we look at
that migration factory,

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:56.850
we can dive a little
bit deeper here

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:59.630
into how we undertake
that migration in scale

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:01.092
and organization.

00:11:01.092 --> 00:11:03.550
So I'll hand over to Ben to
talk a little bit about scaling

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:06.640
that migration factory.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.600
BEN HAGAI: Thank you, Nick.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.520
So now that we have gone through
building the business case,

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:17.450
hopefully getting it approved,
we have a technical approach.

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:21.530
We made sure the technical and
the non-technical foundations

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:22.830
are ready.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:26.030
It's time to get into
the actual migration.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:28.880
For the actual
migration execution,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:33.940
we often see enterprises adopt
the Migration Factory Program.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:39.050
The Migration Factory teams will
manage the overall migration

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:40.920
process end to end.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.490
The applications will
be migrated in what's

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:46.080
called migration waves.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:51.830
The program and approach is
primarily driven top-down

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:55.730
within the organization,
and the factory's aim

00:11:55.730 --> 00:12:01.260
is to maintain and accelerate
the migration velocity, running

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:04.190
parallel to migrate faster.

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:09.770
While we have arrived to an
overall technical approach,

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:13.040
each application will need
to have a detailed discovery

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:18.100
and assessment to make sure
nothing unexpected comes up.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:23.360
And this is why you see in
each of these migration factory

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.950
waves, we have an
assess and plan stages

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:30.140
for their specific
applications being

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:33.470
migrated in that specific wave.

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:39.350
To get started, you will build a
small and agile pilot migration

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:40.580
team.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:43.040
We usually call
the migration team

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:46.340
a pod or a migration squad.

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.680
It needs to have
a representation

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:52.190
from the key people
that will be needed

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:55.400
or from the key teams that
will be needed to achieve

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:57.380
a successful migration.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:03.570
Think application engineers,
IT, product, business people,

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:05.350
et cetera.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:10.720
To scale, and as you mature,
some of these functions

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:14.110
will move from the
migration party itself

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:19.810
to the larger extended migration
team or to the large COE.

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:24.550
Functions like security,
engineering, business,

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:29.620
now doesn't have to be inside
a specific migration pod.

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:33.400
And eventually, we see those
migration pods and the maturity

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:39.400
of the migration execution work
driven by IT and operations.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:41.500
Application migration planning.

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:44.560
This is one of the
most important tasks

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:47.150
for the migration team.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:53.100
Without proper planning, the
migration might be at risk.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:56.890
We generally see
large organizations

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:00.290
re-hosting about 80%
of their workloads

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:05.180
initially, focusing their
transformation efforts in areas

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.940
where they can maximize impact.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:14.570
But keep in mind that workloads
that have already moved to GCP,

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:18.740
even if they were moved
with a simple re-hosting,

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:21.770
can later on be
modernized to leverage

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:27.780
more Cloud-native services
in a much easier way.

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:31.590
The plan you build for the
specific application migration

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:35.880
or application component
migration at this stage

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:39.840
might be defer from the
default path, and even

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:43.860
within the default
migration strategy

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:47.520
that you have defined,
the various patterns that

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:48.570
can be used.

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:53.460
For example, if you
chose re-hosting, OK,

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:57.210
that might be re-hosting
into Google Compute Engine,

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:01.820
GCE, re-hosting into Google
Cloud [INAUDIBLE] engine,

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:05.520
GCVE, re-hosting
with OS upgrade,

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:08.920
re-hosting to Bare
Metal, et cetera.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.790
Defining of a
specific application,

00:15:11.790 --> 00:15:14.160
application component,
migration path,

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:18.120
is the primary outcome
of the base phase.

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:21.470
You should take the overall
strategy for the project--

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:24.780
for the program together
with the specific application

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:30.170
data points achieved through
automated discovery, CMDB,

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:33.210
and information from
key stakeholders

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:36.750
gathered through interviews
and questionnaires

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:42.010
to decide what's the best
path for this application.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:46.050
We continue by going into the
next level of technical details

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:47.670
on the migration.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:50.790
How will this specific
application migration

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:52.170
be executed?

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:55.590
Breaking down to
more and more details

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:57.870
until we are
comfortable that we have

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:02.670
a well-defined viewpoint
on how this migration is

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.170
going to take place.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:08.760
Think pre- and post-migration
activities, testing strategy,

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:13.110
automation that should be
prepared in advance, et cetera.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:16.980
In parallel to this
technical deep dive,

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:21.540
you should plan also further
planning on the migration

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:24.460
timing, taking
into consideration

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:28.750
technical dependencies as
well as business dependencies,

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:32.500
like change freezes,
planned releases,

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:36.310
with the goal of ultimately
defining to the greatest

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:40.450
possible degree when exactly
is a base application going

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:42.630
to be migrated.

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:46.860
Identifying what will be the
first workload to migrate

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:48.660
is critical.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.360
I can't stress
enough how important

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.560
it is to get it right.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:55.320
And when I say
getting it right, it

00:16:55.320 --> 00:17:00.090
doesn't only mean a workload
to be migrated successfully.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:02.550
You want it to be
a workload that

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:06.849
is a good representation of
your typical business workload,

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:09.720
not too simple, an
application that you

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:14.780
can build up documentation
and learn lessons from.

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:16.040
Think back.

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:21.319
What was the main migration
goals of the project?

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:27.010
Ideally, a first mover
that will show the value

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:29.010
correlated to these goals.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:31.460
It can be improved performance.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:33.830
It can be lower cost.

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:36.860
Something that is
measurable and will

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:40.090
make it worthwhile for the
business to stay engaged.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:45.080
You can see various
other considerations.

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:48.200
And a lot can be
discussed on this topic,

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:52.670
but if I had to summarize
it in one sentence,

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:57.110
a good first mover will
have a high business value

00:17:57.110 --> 00:18:00.360
but low effort to implement.

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:04.110
And when we talk about
low effort to implement,

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:08.680
automated tools are here
to help you with that.

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:13.470
They can assign a migration
fit score automatically,

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:17.280
taking into consideration
various factors like operating

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:24.240
system, performance, age, role,
dependencies count, et cetera.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:29.310
We have talked a lot about
people and processes.

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:33.690
But the migration factory cannot
be complete without a very

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:37.240
mature technical angle.

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:41.260
Google has a variety
of technologies

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:43.960
to accelerate
migration of workloads

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:46.690
with an emphasis on automation.

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:49.660
Our migration
factory methodology

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:53.260
works alongside
tools such as Migrate

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:56.950
for Compute Engine,
Migrate for Anthos,

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:59.770
or data migration tools.

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:03.530
Through our tools
and technologies,

00:19:03.530 --> 00:19:07.300
we seek to maximize
value to the business

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:12.250
through accelerating your
migration while minimizing

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:14.110
risk and complexity.

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:19.120
Migrate for Compute Engine will
be the factory's primary tool

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:22.080
for the migration execution.

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:27.390
It's built from the ground up
to enable accelerated migration

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:32.520
of VMs, physical servers, and
cloud machines into Google

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:34.480
Compute Engine.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:37.330
It has all the
essential capabilities

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:40.050
to support the
factory, from built

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:44.410
in testing for your
VMs, automation tools

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:47.980
to drive mass migration
with minimal effort,

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:53.050
advanced networking, security
features, safe and fully

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:58.600
consistent migration and
rollback capabilities,

00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:04.480
and it can even upgrade
Legacy Windows 2008 on the go.

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:09.910
With Migrate for Anthos,
customers can now automatically

00:20:09.910 --> 00:20:13.240
migrate and modernize
existing workloads

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:19.270
running on prem, on other
clouds, or even GCE VMs

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:25.180
directly into containers
running on GKE or Anthos.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:28.270
It's a fantastic
tool to those who

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:33.250
wish to migrate and
modernize, benefiting

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:36.760
from cost efficiencies,
benefiting

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:41.170
from modernized operations
management with the GKE

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:45.820
and Anthos advanced tools
like Service [? Mash, ?]

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:52.210
Tech Driver, and the Ecosystem,
benefiting from modernized app

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:57.340
lifecycle management for
maintenance, upgrades,

00:20:57.340 --> 00:21:01.990
patching, overall,
increasing agility

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:06.560
for operations and developers.

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:09.440
Through the use of
Migrate for Anthos,

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:13.980
you can avoid some of the
common modernization challenges,

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:17.840
especially for those apps that
are not actively developed

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:22.220
or that you don't have access
to the source code, apps

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:28.290
that you want to modernize
fast without rewriting.

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:33.000
Optimization is always
a constant goal,

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:36.750
whether it is as simple as
computer optimization, where

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:41.040
we have numerous tools and
capabilities to help you reduce

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:49.580
costs, optimize performance and
operations, dozens of managed

00:21:49.580 --> 00:21:53.240
services offerings that
can help you modernize

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:59.120
your applications incrementally
or overall, or application

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:01.880
modernization methodologies
and frameworks

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:06.320
for either existing or new apps.

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:09.920
All the way through
easy to use AI and ML

00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:14.450
services that will allow you
to further embed innovation

00:22:14.450 --> 00:22:16.370
faster.

00:22:16.370 --> 00:22:20.170
So when we think on what
are some of the biggest

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:26.540
lessons learned from large scale
Migration Factory programs,

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:29.620
I think the first one
would be in that Cloud

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:31.690
means things can be redone.

00:22:31.690 --> 00:22:33.970
Don't overplan.

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:37.210
One of the fundamental
benefits that we talked about

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:41.380
with the factory approach
is the agile execution,

00:22:41.380 --> 00:22:44.200
the constant improvement.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:45.880
Leverage that.

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:50.770
Focus on automation from the
foundations to migration,

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:56.270
deployment, controls,
automation is there to help you.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:23:02.120
Testing both before and after,
have a well-defined view

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:06.890
of what you want to test and
who is going to do the testing,

00:23:06.890 --> 00:23:11.590
and make sure you execute
it according to that plan.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:14.920
Maintaining velocity is key.

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:20.140
Expect apps to move up
and down the backlog.

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:23.160
Be prepared for that.

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:26.300
And you can never have
too much communications

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:28.640
and business engagement.

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:32.240
Never underestimate the
meaning of those status

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:38.030
reports, status meetings,
and ongoing regular updates,

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:41.960
for the success of
the migration program.

00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:43.720
We are in it together.

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:47.590
Now that you have learned more
about how to make the move,

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:51.700
how to build and scale
your Migration Factory,

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:55.670
you're likely asking
how you can get started.

00:23:55.670 --> 00:24:00.160
Most enterprises choose not to
make this change alone and seek

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:04.540
for a partner to help them
with this migration journey.

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:06.940
At Google Cloud,
we have a variety

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:10.690
of ways to help you in
this journey, including

00:24:10.690 --> 00:24:14.230
a broad and deep
network of partners,

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:18.910
our own specialist professional
services organization that

00:24:18.910 --> 00:24:20.890
is working together
with this partner

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:24.760
ecosystem, a
comprehensive training

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:30.790
and enablement solutions,
and various documentations

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:35.870
to help you answer your
questions along the way.

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:42.370
So to recap, start with
building your business case.

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:47.830
Address current and future
costs as well as value and value

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:49.720
drivers.

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:55.450
Make sure it's driven by facts
rather than just assumptions.

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:59.860
Seek for a wide consensus to
drive your migration journey

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:01.360
forward.

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:04.510
Establish enterprise
foundations that

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:09.010
will allow you to scale
quickly both technical and

00:25:09.010 --> 00:25:15.030
non-technical foundations, like
the Cloud Center of Excellence.

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:17.530
Scale your migration
factory, leveraging

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:19.330
the agile methodologies.

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:23.510
Iterate and improve
your efficiency.

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:27.450
Use the right tools
to migrate quickly.

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:30.050
And if you want
to learn more, we

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:34.640
have a collection of very
thoughtful white paper that

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:40.400
go further into the details
on data center transformation,

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:46.430
building a large scale
Migration Factory, et cetera.

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:50.630
Or, of course, feel
free to reach out

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:54.250
to your Google representative.

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:56.180
Thank you very much.

